{
  "dom_id": "step-q221732",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Consider the following false statement.\n\nIf $a\\ge 7,$ then $a\\ge 9$ for any $a\\in Z.$\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-2}}.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "By denoting $P(a)$ and $Q(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a):\\,a\\ge 7$\n\n- $Q(a):\\,a\\ge 9$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nA *counterexample* is a particular case that disproves the statement. Equivalently, it's a case that proves the negation is true.\n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(a\\ge 7)\\,∧\\,(a<9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\na\\ge 7\\,\\text{and}\\,a<9.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
